WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.837
Claude traces itself, and how not to count twice.

2
00:00:03.117 --> 00:00:05.656
You may not need to write that wrapper for every call.

3
00:00:05.936 --> 00:00:17.051
Anthropic’s SDKs and the Claude API can emit OpenTelemetry spans themselves; the feature is in beta at the time of writing, so its span names and options can change.

4
00:00:17.331 --> 00:00:18.461
There are two halves.

5
00:00:18.741 --> 00:00:31.179
SDK tracing runs in your process and exports through your own OpenTelemetry setup: register a tracer provider and the SDK’s spans appear, nested under whatever span is current.

6
00:00:31.459 --> 00:00:47.587
API tracing is emitted by Anthropic’s servers and delivered to an OTLP endpoint that an organization admin configures; it can see things your process can’t, such as each model request inside one API call and the server tools the API ran.

7
00:00:47.867 --> 00:00:54.757
Both use the GenAI attribute names, so backends that understand the conventions read them without mapping.

8
00:00:55.037 --> 00:00:59.667
Two of Anthropic’s design choices are worth copying in your own instrumentation.

9
00:00:59.947 --> 00:01:16.011
Content is off by default: the default mode is “metadata only” (ids, timing, status, token counts), and “full content” is a deliberate switch whose docs remind you that your backend then stores prompts under its own access and retention settings.

10
00:01:16.291 --> 00:01:18.723
And the docs spell out how to count tokens once.

11
00:01:19.003 --> 00:01:33.360
A request traced by both halves has an SDK span (kind Client) and an API span (kind Server) with the same name and overlapping usage; sum one source per request, not every span that carries a token count.

12
00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:44.072
The same trap appears when you wrap an SDK-traced call in your own chat span: decide which span is the source of truth for cost, and make your dashboard query only that.

13
00:01:44.352 --> 00:01:52.949
Other providers have their own instrumentation, often as OpenTelemetry instrumentation libraries that patch the provider’s SDK.

14
00:01:53.229 --> 00:02:03.256
Check each provider’s current docs, and check that you don’t run a patching library and the SDK’s built-in tracing at the same time, or every call appears twice.
